\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The Pauli symbol}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Take #{n\ge 2}, a complex space of dimension #{2}, a map #{f:R\to\mathbb R^3}, interpolated barycentrically on each grid simplex, and
#{\mathcal B(r)=\sum_{j=1}^3f_j(r)\sigma_j} with the ordered Pauli matrices #{\sigma_j} of \ref{fcap-002L}. Let #{\Sigma} be a
\vocabk{closed surface}{fdip-015O} on no triangle of which #{\tilde f} vanishes. Then every triangle is
regular, #{q=1} at its vertices, and
##{\operatorname{chir}(\Sigma)=-\frac{1}{4\pi}\sum_t c_t\Omega_t,}
where #{\Omega_t} is the signed solid angle at the origin of #{\mathbb R^3} of the triangle with
vertices #{f(v_0),f(v_1),f(v_2)}. Equivalently, this is minus the signed intersection count of its image with a generic ray
from the origin. If #{n\ge 3} and #{\sigma=(v_0,v_1,v_2,v_3)} is an oriented
simplex with no zero of #{\tilde f} on #{\partial\sigma}, then #{\operatorname{chir}(\partial\sigma)} is
#{-\operatorname{sgn}\det(b_1-b_0,b_2-b_0,b_3-b_0)}, with #{b_j=f(v_j)}, when #{\tilde f} has a zero in
#{\sigma}, and #{0} otherwise.}

\proof{
\p{On a triangle take the point #{b} nearest zero in the convex hull of
its three image vertices. For every point #{a} of this hull,
minimizing along #{b+s(a-b)} gives #{b\cdot a\ge |b|^2>0}.
Thus the three normalized directions #{u,v,w} lie in a common open
hemisphere. Normalizing the vertices preserves their positive cone.
For independent directions each covered ray meets the affine triangle
once, with orientation sign #{\operatorname{sgn}\det(u,v,w)} from its
oriented normal paired with the intersection point. The negative line for direction #{u} has projector
#{P_u=(\bbI -u\cdot\sigma)/2}. Multiplying the rank-one projectors
shows that #{\trace(P_uP_vP_w)} is the cyclic overlap product. Using
#{(u\cdot\sigma)(v\cdot\sigma)=(u\cdot v)\bbI+\ui(u\times v)\cdot\sigma}
and the zero Pauli traces gives
##{4\trace(P_uP_vP_w)=1+u\cdot v+v\cdot w+w\cdot u-\ui\det(u,v,w).}
By \ref{fdip-0160} its argument is #{-\Omega_t/2} and it avoids
the cut. Summing the angles gives the formula.}

\p{Cut the sphere along all great circles containing the projected
triangle edges of positive length. Away from the edges, count covering
triangles with their radial orientation signs and coefficients.
This is their signed ray intersection count. Across an open chamber
wall its change is the oriented boundary multiplicity on that wall:
an oriented triangle contributes opposite jumps on entering and leaving.
Subdivide walls at edge endpoints and intersections. Collapsed triangles
have zero area and their boundary arcs cancel on subdivision, since a
closed path along an interval crosses each interior point equally often
both ways. The original chain is closed, so the change is zero everywhere.
A path avoiding the finitely many wall vertices and crossing transversely
joins any two chambers. Summing chamber areas gives total signed area
#{4\pi} times the common count.}

\p{For an invertible affine tetrahedral image the outward boundary gives
one more exit than entry along a generic ray starting inside, and equal
numbers outside. The ordered tetrahedron multiplies this count by its
determinant sign. For a singular linear part, an interior zero would
extend along a kernel line to a forbidden boundary zero. Its image
lies in a plane. Choose a ray parallel to a plane missing zero or
transverse to a plane through zero. It and nearby generic rays miss the image. Thus its
count is zero.}
}
